Date: Tue, 21 Sep 93 15:31:12 -0700
From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Subject: Recovering Objectivist Seeks Source Material

   > [ Kennita says she is not designed to be an Objectivist ... ]

     I'd be curious as to what philosophies do appeal to you, and as to
   the way in which you became aware of your anti-Objectivist tendencies.
   (Not that you have to talk about either just because someone asks.)

Quite so.  However, I may get clearer on the whole problem as I talk.

Re what philosophies appeal to me, I'm not sure yet.  I haven't read
much non-Objectivist philosophy, and I'm busy tiptoeing around my
tendency to believe that to consider any other philosophy would be
morally wrong (since, obviously, I have derived the correct answer
from first principles, thus to abandon it would be irrational and
therefore evil).  I did time as a Catholic (teenage rebelliousness and
Pascal's Wager) and a Pagan (love of ritual and social acceptance),
but I kept snapping back.

Re how I became aware of my anti-Objectivist tendencies:  basically,
just recently it became crystal clear that I'm too emotional to be an
Objectivist, and my emotions are often not connected with any
intellectual/moral assessment of the thing at hand.  I think a lot of
my internal conflict comes from the disagreement between what I feel
and what I think I should be feeling.

Some symptoms that I have connected with my unconscious acceptance of
Objectivist philosophy (not that there's no other way these could have
come about, or that anybody else in a similar situation would suffer
from the same symptoms, but they seem to fall into place in my case):
        1) insecurity in relationships
        2) overriding desire to do things "right"
        3) obsession with having people I respect respect me
        4) reluctance to break with my own established opinions (which
           they approve of) out of fear of losing that respect
        5) desire to find the _one_ right answer to _every_ question
        6) fear of giving the wrong answer to a question
        7) oversimplifying life situations
        8) becoming frustrated when the number of variables in a
           problem grows too much
        9) feeling stupid when I become lost in a chain of reasoning
        10) continuing to fight lost causes because of the way
            things "should be"
        11) assuming that other people want to listen to me because
            I know the right answer
        12) constant mental machinations, even in situations where
            they might generally be considered counterproductive

That will do for the time being.

     In case anyone sends you EMail with "study materials", I'd be
   interested in seeing a bibliography.

I'll certainly be compiling one -- not many entries yet.

BTW -- until I loosen up on myself, I'm not going to be able to do
much of this.  This message has taken an hour and a half (and that's
with 3/4 of the symptom list already mostly prepared from a message to
my mom) and I still haven't rewritten it to my satisfaction, but I
need to get back to work.  If Perry took this long with each of his 
messages, we'd need to shoehorn more hours into the day :-) .

Thanks,
Kennita


