>Not to change the subject back to the Subject, but I have 1 question. Why does
>the DC-X need to hover, move horizontally, and land vertically? This seems to
>be rather extravagant behavior, but please excuse my astounding wealth of
>ignorance.

It needs to hover because it adds flexibility to the vertical
landing option. It doesn't need to land vertically, but then
again it doesn't need to fly in the first place. Since a 
rocket's weight when it's almost empty is a small fraction
of its liftoff weight, and if it's an SSTO that hasn't dropped
any engines, it has a very high probability for being able
to safely land on its butt. This landing mode may actually be
safer for it than the shuttle's, which is apparently a lot
more risky than it looks. If you've been paying attention,
you'll have noticed a great deal of landing delays for what
is here in the SE United States normal weather.


