Date: Tue, 21 Sep 93 23:45:11 EST
From: wit@MIT.EDU (paul whitmore)
Subject: PHIL: Recovering Objectivist

i thought everyone here was a recovering randian (or are some still in 
denial?) i also don't want to provoke a debate on whether A=A. reading 
ayn rand was real mind food. so what now?

Robert Nozick is somebody to read, never mentioned on this list. He's 
thought deeply about libertarian issues, is incredibly clever, and 
inspite of being a Harvard philosophy professor, took the time to write 
an article "On the Randian Argument". In academic circles, this took 
real integrity; he had to first claim that Rand should be taken 
seriously as a philosopher, before he went on to evaluate the 
inconsistency in her claims.

I just looked up the cite (The Personalist, v52, Spring 1971:282-304), 
but if it is hard to reach, run out and get his book _Anarchy State and 
Utopia_. (a libertarian tract, as if immanuel kant had decided to write 
a standup comedy routine)

I don't know how far from Objectivism you want to land. Your own posts 
are clearly argued, and i assume that "recovery" (toyed with above) just 
means you are looking for clear, succinct, solid arguments that range 
over complex ideas. 

Nozick is probably the best source to begin with, b/c he does argue with 
Rand, rather than ignore her. If you like ASU, maybe the list could 
consider discussing some of his arguments--such as his thought 
experiment, The Experience Machine, which provokes an interesting 
perspective on VR and possibly the quality of life as an upload.


