Subject:  Being w/Smart People

i also like to be around funny people, beautiful people, wealthy and 
generous people.

are all of these parasitic? do they promote the life i want to realize?

desire to be around intelligence is probably an s-curved function of the 
individual's intelligence. as much as i value intelligence, it can often 
be treated as convertible with other forms of power. [e.g., if someone 
is a "dumb" polyglot, and i need a translation, i wd prefer access to 
their stored knowledge over a quick wit that doesnt know the language i 
need translated]

more on the S-curve: after a certain point, i do not feel that more is 
more. many great intellects are handicapped by the Tom Sawyer tendency 
to figure out from early on how to avoid hard work. also, many very 
smart people are plain obnoxious egomaniacs. in which case, i wd rather 
read than converse with them.

MIT has an organization, SPAMIT {Stupid People at MIT}. i didn't always 
*get* this club, and i am not a member even today [i have a guest 
account on athena, as a cross-registered harvard student] but most of 
the smartest people i have ever met were at MIT.
 
follow this thread: 
1) i want to be around intelligent people 
2) the more intelligence, the better.  From that, i temporarily ended in 
3) i shd stop producing, and just empty myself for more intelligent 
people to fill with their thoughts.

i don't believe i am an egotist in need of delusions about my 
brainpower, but this glorification of pure intelligence did lead me to 
temporary despair and depression. Even reading this list, i sometimes 
feel, *christ on a bicycle, i cd never write so many intricate arguments 
as some of the people who post here.*

There are interesting reasons why no single intellect can solve all 
problems. In biology, there is no superniche occupier that can displace 
all other organisms; in economics, the law of comparative advantage.

Nevertheless, i want to call attention to the danger in glorifying the 
sheer intellect. As Emo Williams said, "i used to think my brain was my 
most important organ. Then one day i thought, but yeah, look who's 
telling me that." 

